{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>He shall not pour oil upon it.</b> Even though מִנְחָה is feminine, nevertheless it uses the masculine form ״עָלָיו״, because it refers to the קֶמַח (flour), which is masculine. And He commanded to take קֶמַח specifically, because immediately after grinding it becomes קֶמַח, as it is written (Isaiah 47:2): ״וְטַחֲנִי קָמַח״, 'and grind meal.' And we find in this matter as well the language of grinding, as it is written (Judges 16:21): ״וַיְהִי טוֹחֵן בְּבֵית הָאֲסוּרִים״, 'and he was grinding in the prison house,' and it is written (Job 31:10): ״תִּטְחַן לְאַחֵר אִשְׁתִּי״, 'my wife shall grind for another.' Therefore it says ״כִּי מִנְחַת קְנָאוֹת הוּא״ — that is, the flour that comes after grinding; and this meal-offering recalls sin, namely that it recalls the sin of the woman's grinding. And as for what the Ramban asked concerning what is written here ״מִנְחַת קְנָאוֹת הוּא״, and afterward it says ״מִנְחַת קְנָאוֹת הִיא״ — it seems that this is no difficulty at all, for here it speaks at the time when the meal-offering is in the husband's hand, as it is written: ״וְהֵבִיא אֶת קָרְבָּנוֹ״ etc.; it writes ״הוּא״ in the masculine, for in his hand is the staff of his wrath (Isaiah 10:5). But afterward, when it says ״וְנָתַן עַל כַּפֶּיהָ אֵת מִנְחַת הַזִּכָּרוֹן״, it writes ״מִנְחַת קְנָאוֹת הִיא״, for in her hand is the staff of her wrath. And according to its plain sense, in this verse the קֶמַח is not mentioned; therefore it says ״הִיא״, referring back to the מִנְחָה."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:51.591546160Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}